%U A399609 4,2,2,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,0,12,4,3,2,2,2,1,1,1,1,1,1,0
%N A399609 Table of T(n, k) read by antidiagonals: k-dimensional simplex coordinate; repeat j exactly binomial(j+k-1, k-1) times, for n >= 0, k >= 1.
%C A399609 This 2-dimensional array T(n, k) represents the geometric inverse coordinates of multi-dimensional simplex numbers.
%C A399609 The group (or regime) boundaries are defined by the cumulative sum binomial(x+k, k), since j repeats binomial(j+k-1, k-1) times. Solving binomial(x+k, k) = n+1 for its maximum real root yields a direct, non-iterative formula.
%C A399609 In the Frame-Stewart Numbers of the p-peg Tower of Hanoi, the n-th disk's group index is m = T(n-1, p-2) + 1 (n >= 1 p >= 3). Its difference sequence (A397675) repeats 2^K exactly binomial(K+p-3, p-3) times (K = m - 1). For 4 pegs, the difference sequence of A007664 is A137688.
%D A399609 Sandi Klavžar and Uroš Milutinović. "Simple explicit formulas for the Frame-Stewart numbers." Annals of Combinatorics 6.2 (2002): 157-167.
%D A399609 Chai Wah Wu, Algorithms for Complementary Sequences, Integers (2025) Vol. 25, Art. No. A95. See p. 22.
%H A399609 Yoshio Okita, <a href="/A399609/b399609.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10011</a>
%H A399609 Yoshio Okita, <a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1p0styfu9paFTqkPKMkE59VpTD_tibd0p/view?usp=sharing">Report1982.pdf</a>, (Unpublished manuscript, 1982).
%H A399609 Yoshio Okita, <a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1Qiu9FlsxiMKoc5ko3kblEPKIiakVd20L/view?usp=sharing">Explicit Formulas for the Frame-Stewart Numbers of the Generalized Tower of Hanoi: A Finite Difference Approach, 2026</a>.
%H A399609 <a href="/index/To#Hanoi">Index entries for sequences related to Towers of Hanoi</a>
%F A399609 T(n, k) = max { m >= 0 : binomial(m+k-1, k) <= n }.
%F A399609 T(n, k) = ceiling(x*), where x* is the maximum real root of binomial(x+k, k) = (x+1)(x+2)...*(x+k) / k! = n+1.
%F A399609 T(n, k) = Sum_{j=1..n+1} floor(2 / (A_j - n + abs(A_j - n) + 2)) - 1, where A_j = binomial(j+k-2, k).
%e A399609 The array T(n, k) begins:
%e A399609   n\k |  1  2  3  4
%e A399609  ------------------
%e A399609   0   |  0  0  0  0
%e A399609   1   |  1  1  1  1
%e A399609   2   |  2  1  1  1
%e A399609   3   |  3  2  1  1
%e A399609   4   |  4  2  2  1
%e A399609   5   |  5  2  2  2
%e A399609   6   |  6  3  2  2
%e A399609   7   |  7  3  2  2
%e A399609   8   |  8  3  2  2
%e A399609   9   |  9  3  2  2
%e A399609 Values are calculated from the maximum real root of binomial(x+k, k) = n+1.
%e A399609 For n = 4 and k = 3, the maximum real root of binomial(x+3, 3) = 5 is x = 1.181... Thus, T(4, 3) = ceiling(1.181...) = 2.
%e A399609 For n = 5 and k = 4, the maximum real root of binomial(x+4, 4) = 6 is x = 1.130... Thus, T(5, 4) = ceiling(1.130...) = 2.
%Y A399609 Cf. A003056 (column k=2), A056556 (column k=3), A127321 (column k=4), A007665, A182058, A137688, A397675.
